ekly I  don't want to speculate too much about  season two what I will say though is  that as far as I've ever been concerned  with this the story of the crane family  is told it's done it's hard to imagine  something scarier than a television  series based on the works of whoremaster  Stephen King Hulu's Castlerock draws on  many of kings locations tales and  characters combined into one creepy  series starring Andre Holland is a  criminal attorney melanie lynskey as a  real estate agent who can read minds and  bill skarsgård as a Shawshank inmate who  claims to be from an alternate dimension  with references to King's works The  Green Mile misery the shining and many  many more Castle Rock seems to capture a  certain signature for that King is known  for its not always jump in your seat  scary but the show runners have lovingly  adapted the mood and feel of Kings work  particularly claim has been given to the  episode The Queen which focuses on a  character played by the fabulous [ __ ]  Spacek it might be a bit more sad than  scary but hey watching sad things alone  isn't a great idea either  would you leave me like many other  popular horror series castle rock is  adopting an anthology format  albeit one with storylines that might  carryover from season to season in July  2018 Kosho runner dustin Thomason told  The Hollywood Reporter each season is  going to be its own self-contained story  beginning middle and end but I think  that just as the books do we want to  surprise viewers with the ways in which  the stories intersect  aside from kitchen appliances coming to  life taking over the world and enslaving  humanity you probably haven't thought  much about how the technology you depend  on has the potential to create your very  own personal hell scape enter black  mirror a British import acquired by  Netflix which aims to torture us with  tech created by Charlie Booker black  mirror tells its tales and single  episode formats kind of like the  twilight zone or